<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#kulak">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kulak</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as kulaks, podkulachnik</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A kulak was a peasant who owned over 3 hectares of land at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, a pejorative term from the 19th century for wealthier peasants who owned land and lent to poorer ones; the class grew after the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> in 1861 and the Stolypin reform. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> branded kulaks class enemies, and the label spread to peasants with only a little more than their neighbours. From 1930 Stalin's campaign to liquidate them as a class seized their farms, executed many and deported others: Soviet archives record 1,803,392 people sent to labour colonies and camps in 1930 and 1931. Estimates of the deaths vary widely.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kulak.jpg" alt="Kulak" width="330" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Credited to U. Druzhelubov. The date of death is impossible to determine therefore PMA is not known. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Forced_collectivization_USSR.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word kulak was first used in the 19th century as a pejorative for wealthier peasants who owned land and offered credit to poorer ones. After the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> in 1861, and again in the Stolypin reform of 1906 to 1914, some peasants became landowners and lenders. Ivan Koshko, governor of Penza, wrote in 1899 that kulaks had exploited the inactivity of state rural banks to force poorer peasants into predatory loans. By 1912 about 16 per cent of peasants held over 3 hectares per male family member, the statistical threshold for prosperous farmers. Such farmers were about 20 per cent of the rural population and produced almost half the grain sold on the market.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Soviet terminology divided the peasants into poor peasants, mid-income peasants and kulaks, with landless seasonal workers as a fourth group. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> regarded the middle peasants as hesitant allies and the kulaks as class enemies, though the legal criteria shifted often after 1917 and officials applied them arbitrarily. Historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-conquest">Robert Conquest</a> argues that peasants with a couple of cows or a few acres more than their neighbours came to be labelled kulaks.</p><p>In May 1929 a decree defined a kulak household by criteria such as permanent hired labour, ownership of a mill or processing equipment, renting out equipment, or trade and moneylending, and in 1930 local authorities were allowed to add criteria. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> in 1930 and 1931 the label podkulachnik, or sub-kulak, was applied to those close to kulaks or who urged others to resist quotas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In August 1918 Lenin sent his Hanging Order, calling for at least one hundred known kulaks to be hanged publicly. In July 1929 Stalin called for an offensive to eliminate the kulaks as a class, and on 30 January 1930 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> approved their dissolution, sorting them into three categories: those to be sent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a>, those to be moved to distant provinces and those to be resettled within their home provinces. Officials seized farms and executed many, and peasants slaughtered millions of animals rather than hand them over.</p><p>Soviet archives, published internationally in 1990, record 1,803,392 people sent to labour colonies and camps in 1930 and 1931, of whom 1,317,022 are recorded as reaching their destinations. Estimates of deaths range from 700,000 in Soviet sources to as many as six million, the figure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleksandr-solzhenitsyn">Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn</a> gave. The famine of 1932 and 1933 complicates the count.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kulak” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1928, in the Soviet Union, he was declared an ally of the priest and the <b>kulak</b>; the traditions were revived after a letter published in Pravda in 1935, and he became the main symbol of the Soviet New Year's holiday, bringing presents at New Year with his…</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li></ul></section>
